"The model: a slab of YBCO with the field along its c axis, seen end on. Each vortex is a line of field carrying exactly one flux quantum, Φ{#} = h/2e = {#} × {#}⁻¹⁵ Wb, surrounded by circulating supercurrents that die away over the penetration depth λ = {#} nm (Harshman et al., Phys. Rev. B {#}, {#}, {#}, muon spin rotation). The core, a few nanometers across, is far smaller than λ, so the London equations hold: a vortex's field is (Φ{#}/{#}πλ²)K0(r/λ) and two vortices repel each other with a force per meter of length f0·K1(r/λ), where f0 = Φ{#}²/({#}πμ{#}λ³) = {#} × {#}⁻⁴ N/m. The field map adds up every vortex's field.",
"Motion: vortices move through the material against a drag proportional to their speed (no inertia), so each moves along the total force on it: the push of its neighbors, the pull of the defects and nothing else. Time is shown only as a rate, because the drag coefficient is not computed. Forces are cut off smoothly at {#}λ, where they have fallen to under {#} % of the push between nearest neighbors.",
"The faces: outside the slab the applied field is a fixed lattice of vortices at its density, standing for the field there. Vortices come in at a face whenever the strip just inside it holds fewer than the applied field asks for, and leave when pushed across a face or when that strip holds more than the applied field asks for (a pinned vortex stays), so the field at each face equals the applied field: the Bean model's boundary condition. The lower critical field, the surface barrier and the slab's demagnetizing effects are left out. The view repeats above and below.",
